---
/*
 * NYC Crash Hotspots: where the data comes from, how intersections are
 * ranked, how "what goes wrong" and the tips are picked, what "hurt" and
 * "killed" mean here, and the limits (DESIGN.md §7).
 */
import { DATASETS, dateRange, sourceUrl } from '../../lib/crashes/config.js';
import { CAUSES, MANEUVERS } from '../../lib/crashes/facts.js';
import { MIN_CORNER_CRASHES, MIN_CORNER_HURT, TOP_CORNERS } from '../../lib/crashes/hotspot-sql.js';
import { WATCH, CORNER } from '../../lib/crashes/hotspot-view.js';
import { date } from '../../lib/format.js';

interface Props {
  dataThrough: string | null;
  builtAt: string | null;
}
const { dataThrough, builtAt } = Astro.props;
const range = dateRange();
---

<section class="dash-section" id="hs-about" aria-labelledby="hs-about-title">
  <header class="section-head">
    <h2 id="hs-about-title">How this works</h2>
  </header>
  <div class="about-body cl-about">
    <h3>Where this comes from</h3>
    <p>
      NYPD crash reports published on NYC Open Data, in two tables:{' '}
      {Object.entries(DATASETS).map(([t, d], i, all) => <><a href={sourceUrl(t)} rel="noopener">{d.name}</a> ({d.id}){i < all.length - 1 ? ', ' : '.'}</>)}{' '}
      This dashboard covers the last year, from {date(range.from)}{dataThrough ? ` to ${date(dataThrough)}` : ''}; older crashes drop off as it’s rebuilt.
      {builtAt && <>We fetch the whole year again every 3 days, so new reports, corrections and deletions all show up (last on {date(builtAt.slice(0, 10))}).{' '}</>}
    </p>

    <h3>How intersections are ranked</h3>
    <p>
      By the number of crashes there in the last year that hurt someone: with “I walk”, crashes that hurt a pedestrian; with
      “I bike”, a cyclist or someone on an e-bike, e-scooter or moped. Ties go to the one with more crashes that
      killed someone. We list up to {TOP_CORNERS}, and only intersections with at least {MIN_CORNER_CRASHES} crashes, {MIN_CORNER_HURT} of them
      hurting someone. “I drive” counts every crash, since a driver can be in any of them, and changes only who the tips speak to.
    </p>

    <h3>How “what goes wrong” and the tips are picked</h3>
    <p>
      For each place we compare the kinds of crash there (the cause police listed, what the vehicles were doing, the kinds of vehicle
      and, unless you picked how you get around, who was hurt) with the same kinds across its borough. A kind is called out when it’s at
      least {WATCH.minRatio}× as common there as across the borough, with at least {WATCH.minLocal} such crashes in an area or {CORNER.minLocal} at
      one intersection. When nothing stands out, we name the most common cause or action if it’s in at least a third of crashes there.
      Each kind comes with one plain safety tip. The tips are general advice, not a finding about any one crash.
    </p>

    <h3>What “hurt” and “killed” mean here</h3>
    <p>
      They’re the counts on the police report. The report doesn’t grade injuries, so “hurt” covers everything from a complaint of
      pain to a life-changing injury, and this dashboard can’t pick out the serious ones. With “I walk” the people killed or hurt
      are only pedestrians, and with “I bike” only cyclists and people on e-bikes, e-scooters and mopeds, not the drivers or
      passengers in the same crashes.
    </p>

    <h3>How crashes are grouped</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Who was hurt</strong> comes from the report’s counts of people hurt or killed: pedestrians; cyclists; people on e-bikes,
      e-scooters and mopeds; motorcyclists; and people in cars, trucks and buses. The report counts drivers and passengers together, so
      a motorist hurt in a crash with a motorcycle counts as a motorcyclist.{' '}
      <strong>Cause</strong> groups the contributing factors police list for the crash and each vehicle
      ({CAUSES.map((c) => c.label.toLowerCase()).join(', ')}). “Unspecified” and factors like “other vehicular” aren’t in any group.{' '}
      <strong>What the driver was doing</strong> groups each vehicle’s action just before the crash
      ({MANEUVERS.map((m) => m.label.toLowerCase()).join('; ')}).{' '}
      <strong>Vehicle</strong> uses the same vehicle kinds as the crash lookup.{' '}
      A crash can be in several groups, so the shares add up to more than 100%.
    </p>

    <h3>Limits</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Only crashes the police reported are here: ones where someone was hurt or killed, or with at least $1,000 of damage.</li>
      <li>Counts aren’t adjusted for traffic. Busy streets and intersections rank higher partly because more people use them.</li>
      <li>Some crashes have no borough or map location on the report. Those count toward NYC’s totals but can’t be placed on a street, in a ZIP or on the map.</li>
      <li>Intersections are the on and cross street the officer wrote down, so crashes near a corner written as one street only aren’t counted there.</li>
      <li>A year at one intersection is a small number of crashes. A corner with none listed isn’t proof it’s safe.</li>
      <li>Reports can be late or amended, so the last few weeks fill in over time.</li>
      <li>Contributing factors are the officer’s judgment at the scene, not a finding of fault.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="cl-note">Spot a problem with the data? <a href="https://data.cityofnewyork.us/d/h9gi-nx95" rel="noopener">Report it on NYC Open Data</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>
